            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house is quite air tight, however we did still identify a number of locations that can be addressed:



** The largest sources are the 5" chimney for the furnace as well as the 5" wide open combustion air pipe located beside the furnace &amp; directed downward - this pipe is likely the main cause of the lower level (and especially the floor) being so cold. With a furnace upgrade to a new condensing furnace (propane or oil) it will eliminate both of these pipes.



** The open &amp; visible locations of the basement header space



** Around the attic hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack").



** The bathroom exhaust fan on the main floor.



** Around the penetration for the dryer hose.



** Around the pot lights in the upstairs kitchen. The air/vapour barrier in the attic likely wasn't sealed adequately when the pot lights were installed.



** Around the window frames of the basement windows.



** Through electrical outlets on the exterior wall. With these you can remove the faceplace and install a foam gasket in behind.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 56 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.3 GJ. Although the dollar savings aren't huge, the increase in comfort for occupants will be very noticeable, for relatively low effort/ expense.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <VentilationSystem>

As outlined in the Air Sealing section of this report, your house is already quite air tight &amp; will become a little tighter as you air seal some of those pesky air leaks and upgrade your furnace (eliminating both the chimney &amp; the open combustion air pipe).



If your house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed.



Savings: Installing an Energy Star certified HRV that is a minimum 75% efficient and includes ECM blower fan motors is estimated to increase your energy costs by $81 per year and increase your annual energy usage by 2.4 GJ. This essentially offsets the other recommended air sealing measure savings, however the ventilation of your house will be better managed &amp; the comfort &amp; indoor air quality for the occupants will be much better.



OPTION:



Another way for you to increase ventilation whenever you feel it is necessary (when indoor air feels stale/stuffy, or when condensation may by gathering on the bottom edge of the new windows), you can easily do this by:



1. Having your heating contractor install an outside fresh air supply pipe connected to the return plenum of the furnace (perhaps re-purpose that combustion air supply pipe), with an integrated inline damper that you can manually adjust



2. Install a timer switch on the main floor bathroom exhaust fan that you can program.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

PROPANE Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric



You currently have a 184 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 1178 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

 FURNACE UPGRADE



Your mechanical contractor has mentioned to you that your furnace is soon in need of replacement. I've presented two options for you to consider based on fuel type - propane or oil. Both options utilize condensing, direct vent technology, and in addition to reducing your heating fuel consumptiuon and costs, they also reduce the air leakage in your home. One of the largest sources of discomfort found in your home was the combustion air supply pipe that is open, and constantly dumping cold, outside air into your mechanical room during the heating season. This is a typical installation style for older furnaces, but by upgrading to a direct vent furnace, the combustion air, never interacts with air in the home. It eliminates that cold air source.



PROPANE FURNACE

Your house is currently heated with an older 81% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled 2 furnace upgrade options for you:



1. An ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

- This upgrade is currently eligible for a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Full program details are outlined on our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



2. An ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

- This upgrade is currently eligible for a $1,600 rebate from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, plus a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



Both of these upgrades will eliminate the need for the current chimney and the 5" open combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room. 



Savings:

1. By replacing your current propane furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 669 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 28.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).



2. By replacing your current propane furnace with a 97% AFUE condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 769 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 29.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $3,100 in rebates from the combined GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP) &amp; the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an older gas or propane fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas or propane fired furnace. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



OIL FURNACE



As mentioned above your house is currently heated with an older 81% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing oil furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 743 per year in energy costs (very similar to propane), decrease your annual energy usage by 28.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program PLUS $3,500 from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $3,500 is available for replacing an oil fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR certified oil fired furnace. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



NOTE: You have not yet applied to Greenerr Homes so the information in this report is for information purposes only and not applicable unless you apply for and are approved for participation in the Greener Homes Program by Natural Resources Canada (NRCan)



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (your kitchen range hood does not vent outside, therefore the 2 x bathroom fans and clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.5 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your pellet stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

       

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer

      

You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs Â Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



